@page "/docs/common/data-api"

@* Data from an API: a provider in the browser sending its DataRequest to your own API or an OData
   endpoint, the filter's text form, and what the server enforces. Split from Data and filters
   (Data.razor), which covers the request, fields, filters in code, operators, case and dates. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="data-api">
    <Lead>
        In a WebAssembly or Auto app, the <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> runs in the browser, so it sends its
        <Code>DataRequest</Code> to an API. The request can travel as a query string, as JSON, or as OData query options.
        On your own server, the request applies only through the fields you declare.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Calling your own API</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The client writes the request into the URL of a GET with <Code>ToQueryString()</Code>, and the server reads it
            back with <Code>DataRequest&lt;T&gt;.Parse</Code>. Then <Code>ToResultAsync</Code>, given the fields you declare,
            applies it to the query. Both sides use the same row type, here <Code>OrderRow</Code> in a project the client
            and the server share:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Orders.razor (client)">@Client</Code>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)" HighlightLines="8,11">@Server</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The query string looks like <Code>sort=-placed&amp;skip=20&amp;take=10&amp;filter=total ge 100</Code> (shown
            unescaped). It adds <Code>total=false</Code> when the component doesn't need the count. To send the request in
            a POST instead, serialize it with <Code>System.Text.Json</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The request names fields by the client's column keys, so the server's fields need the same keys. With the same
            row type they match. A column on a path, such as <Code>o =&gt; o.Customer.Name</Code>, has the key
            <Code>customer.name</Code>, so give it the <Code>SortKey</Code> of the server's field. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/data#fields">Fields</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Filters as text</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A filter has a text form, a subset of OData's <Code>$filter</Code>. It's the form the query string uses for
            <Code>filter=</Code>. A filter's <Code>ToString()</Code> writes it, and <Code>Filter.Parse</Code> reads it:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="text">@Syntax</Code>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Write</th><th>For</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>eq ne gt ge lt le</code></td><td>Comparisons. <code>eq null</code> and <code>ne null</code> are the null checks.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>status in ('Paid','Pending')</code></td><td>Any of the values. <code>not (status in (...))</code> is none of them.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>customer contains 'ada'</code></td><td>Text that contains the value. <code>contains(customer,'ada')</code> works too, as do <code>startswith</code> and <code>endswith</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>between(total,100,500)</code></td><td>A range. Either end can be <code>null</code>, for no limit.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>isempty(email)</code></td><td>No value, or empty text. <code>isnotempty</code> is the opposite.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>date(placed) eq 2026-10-28</code></td><td>On that day.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>and or not ( )</code></td><td>Groups. <code>and</code> binds before <code>or</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>'O''Brien'</code>, <code>-1.5</code>, <code>true</code>, <code>2026-10-28T09:30:00Z</code></td><td>Values: text in single quotes (with a quote doubled), numbers, true or false, dates and times, and Guids.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            Parsing only builds the filter. Names are field keys and values stay values, so
            <Code>name eq 'x'' or 1 eq 1'</Code> looks for that exact text.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Text that isn't in this form doesn't parse, and <Code>TryParse</Code> returns false.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A request read from a query string with such a filter matches nothing. A <Code>skip</Code> or <Code>take</Code> that doesn't parse is left out.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Text ignores case here too, unlike OData's own <Code>contains</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                The text form doesn't write <Code>MatchCase</Code>, so a condition sent this way ignores case on the server. To
                keep <Code>MatchCase</Code>, send the request as JSON. See
                <Anchor Href="docs/common/data#text-and-case">Text and case</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">OData endpoints</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            When the API is an OData endpoint, <Code>ToODataQuery()</Code> writes the request as its query options:
            <Code>$filter</Code>, <Code>$orderby</Code>, <Code>$skip</Code>, <Code>$top</Code>, <Code>$count</Code> and
            <Code>$search</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@OData</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                OData compares text with case. Text that ignores case is written lower-cased on both sides:
                <Code>contains(tolower(customer),'ada')</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>Field paths use <Code>/</Code>: <Code>customer/name</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                Values are written as their fields' types. A Table's request carries its fields. For a request you read with
                <Code>Parse</Code>, pass the fields: <Code>ToODataQuery(fields)</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                Keys are lower case. If the service matches property names with case, give the columns a <Code>SortKey</Code>
                with the service's names.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>OData has no cursor. Pass <Code>request.Cursor</Code> on yourself.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Limits and security</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A request that comes from the browser can ask for anything, so the server decides what it can reach. These rules
            apply when you pass your own fields to <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> or <Code>Apply</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Only declared fields can be reached.</strong> A condition on a key that isn't a filterable field matches
                nothing, and a sort by one is left out. <Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code> declares every public property,
                so read it from a type made for the API, not from an entity with a password hash on it.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Cap the range.</strong> The request sets its own <Code>Take</Code>, or asks for every row without one.
                The server example above allows at most 100 rows.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Large or deep filters match nothing.</strong> A filter with more than 1,000 nodes (conditions, groups
                and nots), or nested more than 64 deep, matches no rows. The text form nests at most 32 deep.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/data">Data and filters</Anchor>: the request and result, fields, filters in code and the operators.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/get-started/webassembly-and-auto">WebAssembly or Auto</Anchor>: setting up an app whose components run in the browser.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Syntax = """
        total ge 100 and (status in ('Paid','Pending') or customer contains 'ada')
        """;

    private const string Client = """
        @inject HttpClient Api

        <Table TItem="OrderRow" ItemsProvider="LoadOrders" PageSize="20">
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Customer" Sortable Filterable />
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Total" Format="C" Sortable Filterable />
        </Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<DataResult<OrderRow>> LoadOrders(DataRequest<OrderRow> request) =>
                (await Api.GetFromJsonAsync<DataResult<OrderRow>>(
                    $"api/orders?{request.ToQueryString()}", request.CancellationToken))!;
        }
        """;

    private const string Server = """
        using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;   // CountAsync, ToListAsync

        app.MapGet("/api/orders", async (HttpContext context, ShopDb db) =>
        {
            var request = DataRequest<OrderRow>.Parse(context.Request.QueryString.Value ?? "")
                with { CancellationToken = context.RequestAborted };
            // At most 100 rows.
            request = request with { Take = Math.Min(request.Take ?? 100, 100) };
            var rows = db.Orders.AsNoTracking()   // an initializer: EF Core filters and sorts on it
                .Select(o => new OrderRow { Id = o.Id, Customer = o.Customer.Name, Total = o.Total });
            return await request.ToResultAsync(rows, DataFields.Of<OrderRow>(),
                count: (q, ct) => q.CountAsync(ct), list: (q, ct) => q.ToListAsync(ct));
        });

        public sealed record OrderRow
        {
            public int Id { get; init; }
            public string Customer { get; init; } = "";
            public decimal Total { get; init; }
        }
        """;

    private const string OData = """
        var url = $"odata/Orders?{request.ToODataQuery()}";
        // Unescaped:
        // $filter=contains(tolower(customer),'ada') and total ge 100
        // &$orderby=placed desc&$skip=20&$top=10&$count=true
        """;
}
